Q: creating a python program which ask the user for a number - even odd A: <p>def analyze_number(user_input):</p>
<pre><code>&quot;&quot;&quot;

Takes a string input, validates it, and returns:

- number (int)

- parity (&quot;Even&quot;/&quot;Odd&quot;)

- list of numbers from 1 to n

&quot;&quot;&quot;

try:

    num = int(user_input)

    parity = &quot;Even&quot; if num % 2 == 0 else &quot;Odd&quot;

    sequence = list(range(1, num + 1))

    return {

        &quot;number&quot;: num,

        &quot;parity&quot;: parity,

        &quot;sequence&quot;: sequence,

        &quot;error&quot;: None

    }

except ValueError:

    return {

        &quot;number&quot;: None,

        &quot;parity&quot;: None,

        &quot;sequence&quot;: None,

        &quot;error&quot;: &quot;Invalid input: Please enter a valid integer.&quot;

    }
</code></pre>
<p>def factorial(n):</p>
<pre><code>&quot;&quot;&quot;

Returns factorial of a number.

Handles negative input safely.

&quot;&quot;&quot;

if n is None:

    return None

if n \&lt; 0:

    return &quot;Factorial is not defined for negative numbers.&quot;

result = 1

for i in range(1, n + 1):

    result \*= i

return result
</code></pre>
<p># ---- Main Execution ----</p>
<p>user_input = input(&quot;Enter a number: &quot;)</p>
<p>analysis = analyze_number(user_input)</p>
<p>if analysis[&quot;error&quot;]:</p>
<pre><code>print(analysis\[&quot;error&quot;\])
</code></pre>
<p>else:</p>
<pre><code>print(f&quot;Number: {analysis\['number'\]}&quot;)

print(f&quot;Parity: {analysis\['parity'\]}&quot;)

print(f&quot;Sequence: {analysis\['sequence'\]}&quot;)



fact = factorial(analysis\[&quot;number&quot;\])

print(f&quot;Factorial: {fact}&quot;)
</code></pre>

Q: Highlight cells in TCalendar A: <p>Certainly. You can override the <code>TCalendar.DrawCell</code> procedure to implement any custom drawing you'd like.</p>
<p>Note, however, that this requires that you have some knowledge about the control's internals.</p>
<p>Here's a small example:</p>
<pre class="lang-pascal prettyprint-override"><code>{ TCalendar }

procedure TCalendar.DrawCell(ACol, ARow: Longint; ARect: TRect;
  AState: TGridDrawState);
begin

  if (ACol mod 3 = 0) and (ARow = 2) and not (gdSelected in AState) then
    Canvas.Brush.Color := clSilver;

  inherited;

end;
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/tCgmdtKy.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/tCgmdtKy.png" alt="Screenshot of TForm with subclassed TCalendar showing three highlighted cells." /></a></p>
<p>In this trivial example, I simply assume that the text will be legible on a <code>clSilver</code> background. In production code, you must not make such assumptions.</p>
<p>Also note that this approach hands you the row and column of a cell, and not a date. You probably want to work with dates instead. To convert between dates and (row, column) pairs, follow the same logic as the rest of the control.</p>
<p>For instance, in the implementation of <code>TCalendar.GetCellText</code> I see that</p>
<pre><code>DayNum := FMonthOffset + ACol + (ARow - 1) * 7;
</code></pre>

Q: How do I import a different implementation of an interface on client vs server in React Router? A: <p>You can define a vite plugin with some refactoring of the two strategies you have:</p>
<p>Add a <code>logger</code> export in both <code>nodeLogger.server.ts</code> &amp; <code>consoleLogger.ts</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>export { nodeLogger as logger };
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>export { consoleLogger as logger };
</code></pre>
<p>In <code>~/util/logger.ts</code> you can simply declare the following:</p>
<pre><code>import type { ILogger } from &quot;~/types/logger&quot;;
export declare const logger: ILogger;
</code></pre>
<p>Finally in your <code>vite.config.ts</code> add a custom plugin using <a href="https://vite.dev/guide/ssr#ssr-specific-plugin-logic" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>ssr option</code></a> to support consumers importing <code>~/util/logger</code>:</p>
<pre><code>function loggerPlugin(): Plugin {
  return {
    name: &quot;logger-resolver&quot;,
    resolveId(id, _importer, options) {
      if (id === &quot;~/util/logger&quot;) {
        return options.ssr
          ? this.resolve(&quot;~/util/nodeLogger.server&quot;)
          : this.resolve(&quot;~/util/consoleLogger&quot;);
      }
    },
  };
}

export default defineConfig({ plugins: [loggerPlugin()] });
</code></pre>
